At the class level, keep the Redis-talking and the algorithm separable so we can swap implementations (in-memory for tests, sliding-window variant for strict tiers).

```java
public interface RateLimiter {
    RateLimitResult tryAcquire(String key);
}

public record RateLimitResult(boolean allowed, long remaining, long retryAfterSec) {}

public class RedisTokenBucketLimiter implements RateLimiter {
    private final RedisScript<List<Long>> script;   // pre-loaded Lua
    private final RedisTemplate<String, String> redis;
    private final TierResolver tierResolver;        // api key -> (capacity, refillRate)

    @Override
    public RateLimitResult tryAcquire(String key) {
        Tier tier = tierResolver.resolve(key);
        List<Long> r = redis.execute(script,
            List.of("rl:" + key),
            String.valueOf(tier.capacity()),
            String.valueOf(tier.refillRate()),
            String.valueOf(System.currentTimeMillis()));
        return new RateLimitResult(r.get(0) == 1L, r.get(1), 1L);
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **Interface-first** — the gateway depends on `RateLimiter`, not on Redis. Tests use an in-memory implementation; we can A/B-test algorithms behind the same contract.
- **`TierResolver` is its own thing** — tier lookup is cached locally (TTL ~30s), not queried from a database per request.
- **`RedisScript` pre-loaded once at startup** — the SHA1 is sent to Redis instead of the full script body on every call (`EVALSHA`).

Three failure scenarios to plan for:

- **Redis unreachable** — network partition, primary failover in progress.
- **Redis slow** — slow query, GC pause, throttled disk.
- **Lua script error** — bad inputs, script eviction from cache.

The default response to all three is **fail open**: log loudly, increment a circuit-breaker counter, allow the request. Reasoning:

- Fail-closed turns a Redis blip into a total API outage — the protection layer becomes the failure layer.
- Fail-open accepts a bounded risk — unthrottled traffic for the duration of the outage, usually seconds.
- Pair with a **circuit breaker** so we stop hammering a dead Redis and back off.

The filter wrapper:

```java
try {
    RateLimitResult r = limiter.tryAcquire(apiKey);
    response.setHeader("X-RateLimit-Remaining", String.valueOf(r.remaining()));
    if (!r.allowed()) {
        response.setHeader("Retry-After", String.valueOf(r.retryAfterSec()));
        response.sendError(429, "Rate limit exceeded");
        return;
    }
} catch (Exception e) {
    log.error("Rate limiter unavailable — failing open", e);
    rateLimiterFailureCounter.increment();
}
chain.doFilter(request, response);
```

**Local fallback for longer outages** — wrap the Redis call in a Resilience4j circuit breaker; on open-circuit, fall back to a per-instance in-memory counter that gives rough rate limiting without shared state. Imperfect, but better than no limiting at all.
